hifiberry-audiocontrol (0.15.1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * audiocontrol exits when it is asked to stop. It never had: every stop,
    restart and package upgrade ended in SIGKILL after systemd's stop timeout,
    so nothing in the process ever got the chance to flush its state. The
    signal was not being ignored -- Rocket received it and shut the HTTP
    server down on time -- but the flag the main thread waits on was only
    cleared from a ctrlc handler that Rocket's own signal handlers replace as
    soon as the server launches. That handler had therefore never run, taking
    with it the force-exit watchdog written to catch exactly this, which sat
    inside it and could not fire. The main loop is now ended by the API server
    returning, which happens precisely when Rocket has finished shutting down.
    A stop takes about six seconds, which is Rocket's own grace and mercy
    periods, rather than the full ten-second timeout followed by a kill.
  * Starting the API server reports which of two things happened rather than a
    bare success. "Rocket ran and has shut down" and "the webserver is
    disabled, nothing was started" were the same value, so the caller had to
    re-read the configuration to tell them apart. Both are now distinct
    outcomes the compiler can check.
  * The startup log no longer claims "API server started on port 1080" from a
    thread that has not started anything. The webserver logs its own address
    when it binds one, which is the code that knows.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Tue, 01 Sep 2026 14:33:56 +0200

hifiberry-audiocontrol (0.15.0) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Image and lyrics paths in every payload now carry the externally visible
    API prefix when audiocontrol is reached through a reverse proxy. Album
    cover art, artist thumbnails and the song paths on the WebSocket
    previously carried the internal path while the now-playing response
    carried the external one, so a client needed one rule per endpoint and
    per transport. A path that fell through the proxy answered 200 with the
    web interface's index page rather than an error.
  * List responses declare Vary: X-Forwarded-Prefix.
  * The library version token varies with the client's API prefix, both in the
    ETags on the album and artist lists and in the library_version field of
    /library/<player>. Without that, a prefixed and an un-prefixed response
    shared one validator for one URL, so a conditional request could be
    answered 304 for a representation the client never held, leaving it with
    paths wrong for the route it was on. The field remains opaque and is still
    compared for equality only.
  * A forwarded prefix that is not a plain path is refused, and paths then go
    out in their internal form exactly as they do when no prefix is reported.
    A value beginning with two slashes made every emitted path
    protocol-relative, so a browser resolved cover art against that host
    instead of the device, and a value carrying a query or fragment marker
    swallowed the rest of each path. Control characters are refused with them:
    a URL parser removes tab, carriage return and line feed before parsing, so
    a value containing one could otherwise be reassembled into either shape
    after passing the check.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Tue, 01 Sep 2026 11:14:47 +0200

hifiberry-audiocontrol (0.14.0) trixie; urgency=medium

  * api: the album and artist list endpoints answer conditional requests. A
    client had no exact way to learn the library changed - the only signal was
    the counts on /library/<player>, so any edit leaving totals unchanged was
    invisible and cache invalidation could only be a heuristic. Against a real
    library the album list is 2.9 MB raw and 799 KB gzipped, so a client opening
    a library browser re-fetched all of it to learn nothing had changed. Both
    endpoints now carry a weak ETag and answer a matching If-None-Match with a
    bodyless 304. A request without If-None-Match returns exactly what it did
    before, so existing clients are unaffected, and a backend that does not
    track changes - LMS today - sends no validator rather than one it cannot
    honour.
  * library: a version counter that moves whenever the library's contents
    change, not merely when it loads. The background updaters write genres and
    artist metadata for up to an hour after a scan, so a version bumped only on
    load would tell a client nothing had changed while the daemon replaced the
    contents underneath it. The artist path bumps only on a real change,
    compared field by field against what the collection already held, so a
    sweep that finds nothing new does not invalidate every client's cache.
  * library: the validator carries a per-instance nonce. A bare counter resets
    to zero on restart and climbs back through values it already issued, so a
    client revalidating while it sat at a previously issued number would be told
    304 for content that had since changed - silently, and for as long as it
    kept that cache. A restart now invalidates every issued token exactly once.
  * api: the version is read before the list is built, not after. The sweep
    mutates the collection and then bumps, on its own thread, so reading the
    other way round could hand a client pre-update data carrying a post-update
    token. Reading it first turns that race into one wasted refetch instead of a
    stale library.
  * api: GET /library/<player> reports the same token, so a client can poll one
    small response rather than issuing a conditional request per list. It is
    opaque - compare it for equality only.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Tue, 01 Sep 2026 07:45:49 +0000

hifiberry-audiocontrol (0.13.0) trixie; urgency=medium

  * images: the size ladder is configurable. It was a compile-time constant, so
    an operator whose clients wanted different sizes had to build the daemon
    from source. `services.images.sizes` and `services.images.prewarm_sizes` in
    audiocontrol.json now set it, both optional and both defaulting to the
    previous values, so an existing installation upgrades unchanged. Bad input
    is sanitised rather than rejected - this hardware has no screen, and
    refusing to boot over a mistyped number would take the music offline for a
    cosmetic error - and every dropped value is named in the log, including a
    value too large for the type, which is refused rather than silently
    wrapped into a plausible-looking smaller one. GET /capabilities reports the
    configured ladder, so a client asks rather than assumes. An empty
    prewarm_sizes is honoured as "pre-warming off" rather than replaced by the
    default.
  * imagecache: the variant purge deletes only sizes the ladder no longer
    offers. Upgrading to 0.12.0 changed the ladder from 100-200-400-800 to
    100-140-200-280-400-800 - a strict superset, every previous size still
    served - and the whole-ladder comparison deleted all 5,714 cached
    thumbnails, which the device then had to regenerate. Adding a size now
    deletes nothing. Size-awareness is also what makes the purge safe to run
    concurrently: one that deletes only retired sizes cannot race the pre-warm
    job into removing a thumbnail it just generated.
  * imagecache: the purge runs in the background instead of blocking startup.
    It ran inside the image cache's initialisation, before the API server bound
    its listener, and held the cache lock for the whole walk; on the 0.12.0
    upgrade the daemon answered nothing for about a minute and nginx returned
    502 throughout, which is indistinguishable from a hang. It now runs on its
    own thread and takes the cache lock only long enough to read a path. The
    ladder marker is written only after a successful purge, so a daemon killed
    part way through retries rather than believing it finished.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 19:35:27 +0000

hifiberry-audiocontrol (0.12.0) trixie; urgency=medium

  * images: add grid rungs at 140, 200 and 280 px. The ladder jumped from
    100 straight to 400, so every client whose grid cell was not exactly
    100 px snapped up to 400: a 100 pt cell on a 2x display asked for 200
    and was served four times the pixels it could show, over a link and a
    decode it paid for in full. The ladder is now 100, 140, 200, 280, 400,
    800 - the grid sizes for 1x, 1.4x, 2x and 2.8x, which is the range of
    display scales clients actually run at. The larger rungs are unchanged;
    they serve single covers, where one image at full quality is the point.
  * imagecache: pre-warm every grid rung rather than one. The background
    walk generated only the 400 px variant; it now generates 140, 200 and
    280 px per album, because the client picks its size from the display
    scale and any of the three may be the first thing asked for. Variants
    that already exist are still skipped, so a restart mid-walk and a rescan
    stay cheap, and the 250 ms throttle moves from per album to per variant
    so the job's duty cycle holds as the rung list widens.
  * imagecache: the new rungs change the ladder fingerprint, so variants
    generated by an earlier release are purged on first start and
    regenerated against the new ladder. This upgrade therefore pays one full
    pre-warm pass over the library; later restarts do not.

