hifiberry-audiocontrol (0.11.1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * auth: allow GET /capabilities without authentication. The endpoint was
    absent from auth.d/audiocontrol-auth.json, so it fell through to the
    manifest's default_tier of risky and nginx answered 401 on a gated
    device. That defeats the endpoint's own purpose: a client is told a 404
    means the daemon does not resize images, but a 401 leaves it unable to
    tell "not supported" from "not allowed", which is exactly the guessing
    the endpoint exists to remove. It sits beside /version in the
    unauthenticated GET tier, since it reports only which image sizes the
    build offers. POST /imagecache/variants/purge deliberately stays gated:
    it deletes files.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 15:08:37 +0000

hifiberry-audiocontrol (0.11.0) trixie; urgency=medium

  * api: serve resized cover art through a size parameter on the library and
    artist image endpoints. Cover art was served at full size only - a mean
    243 KB at 1280x1280 measured over a library of 11,318 albums - while
    clients draw it into 120 pt grid cells. A request snaps up to one of
    100, 200, 400 or 800 px; at 400 the same image is about 19 KB, so a
    client caches roughly 213 MB of thumbnails rather than 2.8 GB of
    originals. Every client pays the old cost, not just new ones: the web UI
    renders the same grids from the same full-size images. A request without
    the parameter returns the original bytes unchanged, so existing clients
    are unaffected, and a size above the image's own resolution serves the
    original rather than upscaling it.
  * api: image responses carry ETag and Cache-Control, and answer
    If-None-Match with 304. They previously carried only Content-Length, so
    every client had to build its own cache with no way to revalidate. The
    policy follows the identifier rather than the endpoint: album art keyed
    by album id gets a year and immutable, while artist images get a day,
    because a user can replace one by uploading a custom image and a year of
    immutability would strand that replacement behind every client cache in
    the field.
  * imagecache: store resized variants beside their originals, and drop them
    when the original changes. A variant is derived data, regenerable at any
    time, so it is named after the original it came from and lives in the
    same directory. Re-storing an original removes its variants first -
    without that a replaced cover would show the old art at grid size and the
    new art at full size, with nothing reporting an error. Cache statistics
    report the derived share separately from the total, and a new
    POST /imagecache/variants/purge reclaims it.
  * imagecache: generate the grid-size thumbnail in the background after a
    library load. Serving on demand alone means the first scroll through a
    freshly scanned library pays an image decode per cell. The walk generates
    only the 400 px size, skips albums that already have one so a rescan is
    nearly free, pauses between albums it actually worked on so it loses to
    playback, and reports through the background job registry like the other
    long-running tasks. A guard prevents a second walk starting on a rescan.
  * api: add a capabilities endpoint. A version string identifies a build but
    does not say what it supports, so a client reasoning from it has to carry
    a table mapping versions to behaviour - and this API has varied underneath
    clients before without saying so. GET /capabilities reports the available
    image sizes; a release answering it with 404 does not resize, which is a
    complete answer a client can act on without probing. Resizing applies to
    album identifiers on players that keep cover art in the image cache, and
    the API documentation says so rather than leaving it to be discovered.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 14:41:07 +0000

hifiberry-audiocontrol (0.10.0) trixie; urgency=medium

  * library: count an artist's albums from the album-artist mapping rather
    than by listing them. The artist list endpoint derived album_count by
    calling get_albums_by_artist_id once per artist, and that call walks
    every album in the library, so serving the list was O(artists x albums).
    On a library of 1,904 artists and 11,318 albums /library/mpd/artists took
    2.2 s for a 282 KB response, while /albums - ten times the payload -
    returned in 0.5 s. The mapping already indexes artist to album ids, so
    the count is a lookup; backends without such an index keep the previous
    behaviour through a default implementation.
  * api: add_tracks queues several tracks in one request. Queueing a
    12-track album needed 14 requests, and partial failure was every
    client's problem - if track 7 failed, the client held a half-built queue
    and had to choose between playing an incomplete album and discarding
    what worked. The new command takes the whole list and maps onto the
    existing plural queue command, so no player backend changed and
    add_track keeps working unchanged.
  * mpd: queue a batch over one connection, and keep its order. Each track
    was added through its own freshly opened connection, so a 12-track album
    opened twelve; and inserting at the front placed every track at position
    0, so each insert pushed the previous one down and the batch landed
    reversed.
  * mpd: stream title splitting no longer fails closed, and can be set per
    station. The split returned nothing when the order lookup was undecided
    or unknown - the permanent state on a device that cannot reach
    MusicBrainz - leaving the title combined and the artist empty. It now
    falls back to the "Artist - Title" reading that streams overwhelmingly
    use. Order and separator can also be set per station through four new
    endpoints, and a set value wins over anything guessed or learned; a set
    separator is tried first, which is what lets "AC/DC - Highway to Hell"
    split on the dash rather than inside the band name. Splitters cached by
    an earlier version still load.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 14:06:14 +0000

hifiberry-audiocontrol (0.9.2) trixie; urgency=medium

  * mpd: emit shuffle_changed and loop_mode_changed. The MPD controller
    subscribed to MPD's Options subsystem but its handler was a stub, so
    toggling shuffle or cycling the loop mode on the default player produced
    no WebSocket event at all - a client subscribed, waited, and nothing
    arrived. Options fires for any option change, consume and crossfade
    included, so the controller now holds the last-seen random/repeat/single
    and emits only on a real transition; the first observation after a
    connect establishes the baseline, so a reconnect does not look like
    someone toggling a setting.
  * postinst: chown /var/lib/audiocontrol recursively. Installs that at some
    point ran audiocontrol as root leave root-owned files under the cache
    directories, which the non-recursive chown did not fix, so the daemon
    still could not overwrite them after an upgrade. /etc/audiocontrol stays
    non-recursive: its dpkg conffiles, some shipped by other packages, remain
    root-owned.
  * control: accept libasound2t64 as an alternative to libasound2, so the
    dependency is satisfiable on trixie.
  * build.sh no longer sources the cross-compile environment; that setup is
    supplied by the surrounding OS build.

